    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "The attachment system does **not** reference-count. GC is storage-pressure-driven:\n\n1. query attachments ordered by `last_accessed_at_utc` ascending;\n2. evict until storage drops below the target threshold;\n3. evicted rows **retain metadata** (`hash`, `mime_type`, \u2026) so the hash stays known;\n4. if the data is needed again, `get()` re-fetches it from a peer through the transport.\n\nThat choice is what makes \"lazy data availability\" (design principle 4) coherent: a ref in document state never depends on this reactor holding the bytes, so dropping bytes is a cache decision rather than a data loss. For mutable backends (disk, S3) eviction removes the files; on immutable backends it stops serving them.\n\nThere is one hard constraint that predates the design: a deployment **without a functioning transport** (for example `NullAttachmentTransport`) must not enable eviction at all \u2014 there would be nothing to restore from.\n\nReservations are cleaned up on a different schedule and by a different mechanism: `IReservationStore.deleteExpired(now?)` deletes every row whose `expires_at_utc` has passed and returns the count, and should run on an interval (hourly is suggested) so aborted or failed uploads do not accumulate in `attachment_reservation`. The default TTL is `DEFAULT_RESERVATION_TTL_MS`, **24 hours**, configurable per store instance.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/reactor/docs/attachments.md` \u2014 Design \u203a Principles (LRU eviction); Garbage Collection\n- `packages/reactor/docs/attachments-hash-first.md` \u2014 Failure modes (no-transport deployments must not enable eviction)\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
